      <p class="sec-aside" style="margin-top:16px">Every assessment produces a finding list, and ours is no exception — the severity summary above is the part that decides whether a finding matters. The full reports go to customers under NDA on request. [verify: remediation plan and target dates for the two medium findings carried over from March 2025, and reconciliation of the internal validation checklist against the July 2026 results - engineering and CISO. Detail deliberately not restated on this page]</p>
    </section>

    {/* ── The security slice of the comparison matrix ────────────────────────
        Damian's ask: everything about security on the security page, including the
        matrices that carry security rows. Rendered from the same COMP data the
        compare hub uses, so a grade cannot say one thing here and another there.
        Static HTML, because a reviewer with script restricted should still see the
        evidence; only the sourcing cards need script to position themselves. */}
    <section class="sect reveal flush" id="compare">
      <div class="kicker">Us against the market</div>
      <h2>Not who has more certificates. Whose certificate covers the box in your room.</h2>
      <p class="sdek">
        A certificate belongs to a company. A room device is a product. Those are not the same
        thing, and the gap between them is where most of this table lives. Three rows below ask
        what the <b>company</b> has &mdash; its ISO certification, its SOC 2, whether the scope is
        even legible. Seven ask what the <b>device</b> does: the operating system on it, whether its
        firmware is signed, whether it can authenticate to your NAC, whether anyone independent has
        ever assessed the appliance itself.
      </p>
      <p class="sdek">
        Of the fourteen vendors here, <b>two publish a company certificate whose scope actually
        names the product</b> &mdash; and in several cases the certificate provably covers something
        else: a cloud service rather than the appliance, a scheduling panel rather than the sharing
        device, a desktop client version from 2021. That is not a gotcha. It is the question a
        security reviewer is really asking when someone hands them a badge, and it is worth asking
        of us too.
      </p>
      <p class="sdek">
        Graded from each vendor&rsquo;s own published documentation and from public certification
        registries &mdash; ANSSI, BSI, the Common Criteria portal, NIST CMVP, FedRAMP, the DoDIN APL
        and CSA STAR &mdash; in August 2026. <b>Click any graded cell</b> for the reasoning, the
        document it came from and the date we read it; the source opens on the vendor&rsquo;s own
        site. Read the assurance rows for what they measure: whether the evidence can be
        <em>retrieved</em>, not whether testing happened. A vendor who tests thoroughly and
        publishes nothing scores badly here and may still be perfectly secure. Several beat us
        outright in places, and the cards say where.
      </p>
      <Fragment set:html={secMatrix()} />

      {/* THE PUBLISH LIST. Yellow because it is a verify flag like every other one
          on this site, and it renders as a real <mark class="vflag"> in the served
          HTML rather than by client-side script, so it survives a print, a scrape
          and JS being off.

          Why it sits HERE, under this table, rather than in a workbook. Every one
          of the 140 competitor cells above now carries a document and a date. The
          Polaris column carries two notes and no source at all — because these
          files do not exist at a public URL yet. That is not a copy problem, it is
          a missing-artefact problem, and it is the one asymmetry a competitor
          answering this page would lead with: we graded them on what they publish,
          having published none of our own primary evidence.

          Delete this block when the files are up, and source the Polaris column in
          the same pass. */}
      <div class="pubneed reveal">
        <mark class="vflag">
